i received an email  from a popular pub restaurant in my  constituency just  giving me these frankly terrifying  figures about what they were going to be  asked to pay for their energy so  i wanted to focus on the fact that  businesses are finding it tough as well  as households yeah i remember speaking  to a pub owner actually on sunday and  just been completely shocked actually  frankly by the kind of increases that  they were going to be seeing and what  did you make of the prime minister's  response well i mean i i welcome the  very clear response that  the package that will be announced  tomorrow will include  help for businesses as well as  households i mean obviously we don't  know what that is yet but it was really  reassuring because of course much of the  debate is understandably about  individuals and families but  we don't want to see sort of mass  business closures as well so we do need  to focus on the energy costs that  they're facing too it's going to be  expensive though isn't it um list trust  has ruled out a windfall tax do you  think that was the right thing to do  well  we already have a windfall tax at the  moment so um it's already the case that  the  the big energy producers  are contributing to  solving this problem and and we have a  progressive tax system so higher earners  and companies will all be playing their  part in  in funding all of this  so i i don't think it was an  unreasonable decision for  for liz to say that she wasn't going to  introduce  a specific new windfall tax but  i i feel it's best to wait for the  detail in the package to see how it will  operate and how it will be funded in  detail yeah we are of course waiting  that aren't we and tomorrow's  announcement and but at the same time if  she's not going to go for her tax risers  then it looks likely it's going to be  funding by a pretty big increase in  borrowing i spoke to philip hammond the  former chancellor of the weekend  and he said that he's worried the  conservatives reputation for fiscal  responsibility and competent governments  at stake do you think that's right  this is this will have an impact on  borrowing in the public finances and so  there are risks i mean that that was  obviously the debate during the  leadership election but this is an  emergency i mean we we haven't seen sort  of an energy price shock on this scale  since the 1970s you know we we are  seeing vladimir putin using energy  prices as a weapon of war  and in times of emergency unfortunately  the public finances  are impacted then there's simply  no way to avoid that if we're going to  do what we have to do but i i'm i feel  confident that our new prime minister  will be looking very carefully at all  the numbers to try and minimize the  damage on the public finances and to  ensure that we can get them back on a  sound footing in the future i guess one  way to get it back onto the sound  footing would be three taxes though  something that she said that she doesn't  want to put up  well she's clearly ruled out um  that she's very clear about the tax  reductions that she want to make so i  i would suspect there's there's no sort  of flexibility on that but as i say yeah  i'm sure  she and her whole new team will be  looking carefully about how this is  funded uh you i'm just interested to  know as well you know  liz trust uh new prime minister you know  if this week of course you know she's  been pointing her new cabinet and she's  had her first promises questions today  what kind of prime minister do you think  she's going to be  i think she's going to be  determined resilient  and she's got real kind of grit and she  she is absolutely determined to deliver  so i  i very much believe and hope that she's  going to be able to do that she  faces these incredibly difficult  circumstances not just  energy price rises not just the war in  ukraine but  the aftershocks have covered on the nhs  as well  but  i think she  performed really strongly at prime  minister's questions and she's got a  long and impressive track record in  government of getting things done so  that's what i i think she's going to do  as prime minister you sat around the  cabinet table with her what what what's  she like  she is she's very engaging she's got a  good sense of humour and the years we  sat in cabinet together  for much of the time she was doing the  defra brief and i remember her staunchly  standing up for the  food and drink industry as one of our  most important sectors  so she's she's always been a very  impressive figure in government and in  parliament you of course didn't back her  in the leadership race you went for  rishi sunac um i have to say you know  looking at the cabinet appointments  it's pretty clear that she's rewarded  those people who were loyal to her like  admittedly there are some risky student  backers when you go further down the  rungs or the ministerial uh ladders when  it comes to the big  decision makers  she hasn't really made the most  inclusive cabinet has she  she's appointed some incredibly able  people to her cabinet and her government  you know we're we're a parliamentary  party with  with a great many very talented and  capable people and i think it's  understandable that um liz wants some of  her key allies in the big positions and  as you say you know she's appointed some  rishi backers and indeed she's appointed  people who directly stood against her as  candidates in the leadership elections  so in a way the people that she relied  on for their votes  i think so but i i think she's got she  has got a great team um and that that  includes  conserva mps from from all wings of the  party and i i sincerely wish them well  you know it is it just as we've been  discussing they face an incredibly  difficult task as difficult as any  government that's taken office  in decades

